{
  "id": "compression-amoebots",
  "model": "programmable-matter",
  "name": "Compression",
  "origin": {
    "ref": "Cannon2016Compression",
    "year": 2016,
    "note": "PODC 2016."
  },
  "summary": "Particles following a local stochastic rule must gather into a configuration with small perimeter.",
  "statement": "Starting connected, particles perform a Markov chain with bias $\\lambda$ towards having more neighbours. The system is $\\alpha$-compressed if its perimeter is at most $\\alpha$ times the minimum possible.",
  "primer": {
    "intro": "Picture a crowd of penguins huddling together for warmth, each one just shuffling towards places with more neighbours. Here $n$ particles start as a connected group on the triangular grid. A particle sits on one node of an endless triangular grid and moves in two steps: it *expands* into an empty neighbouring node, briefly occupying both, then *contracts* into one of the two; so one expansion and one contraction move it by one node. One particle at a time, chosen at random, makes a random move; particles have no names, a few bits of memory, and no compass. The goal is to become *compressed*: the *perimeter* (the length of the walk around the outside of the group) should be at most $\\alpha$ times the smallest possible perimeter for $n$ particles, for a constant $\\alpha > 1$. The question is not how fast this happens, but whether, after running long enough, the group is compressed with very high probability.",
    "ideas": [
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Move at random, but prefer more neighbours",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "Cannon2016Compression",
        "idea": "Fix a number $\\lambda > 0$, the *bias*. A randomly chosen particle does the following.\\n\\n1. Pick one of its six directions at random. If that node is empty, expand into it.\\n2. Count $e$, its neighbours at the old node, and $e'$, the neighbours it would have at the new node.\\n3. Check, by looking only at nearby nodes, that the move would neither split the group nor enclose a hole. If it would, contract back.\\n4. Otherwise contract into the new node with probability $\\lambda^{e'-e}$ (or with certainty if this number exceeds 1); else contract back.\\n\\nSo moves that gain neighbours are always accepted when $\\lambda > 1$, and moves that lose neighbours are accepted only sometimes.",
        "analysis": "In the long run, each shape appears with probability proportional to $\\lambda$ raised to its number of touching pairs, and fewer touching pairs means a longer perimeter. For a large bias, $\\lambda > 2+\\sqrt2 \\approx 3.41$, the group is compressed with all but exponentially small probability [Cannon2016Compression]. For a small bias, $\\lambda < 2.17$, it is the opposite: the group stays stretched out, with perimeter a constant fraction of the largest possible, even though each particle still prefers neighbours. Between $2.17$ and $3.41$ the paper's theorems say nothing.",
        "why_it_matters": "This 'local random rule with a bias' recipe became the template for separation, alignment and bridging."
      },
      {
        "kind": "lower-bound",
        "title": "Too many stretched shapes: why a weak bias fails",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "Cannon2016Compression",
        "idea": "Two forces compete. The bias favours each single compact shape over each single stretched one: a shape with perimeter $p$ gets weight roughly $\\lambda^{-p}$. But stretched shapes are far more numerous. The number of shapes whose boundary has length $p$ grows roughly like $(2+\\sqrt2)^p$, because a boundary is a walk that never crosses itself on the honeycomb grid, and such walks grow at exactly that rate. Multiply: all shapes of perimeter $p$ together weigh about $\\left((2+\\sqrt2)/\\lambda\\right)^p$.",
        "analysis": "If $\\lambda > 2+\\sqrt2$, this total shrinks exponentially as $p$ grows, so long perimeters are very unlikely and the group compresses. If $\\lambda$ is small, the sheer number of long-perimeter shapes outweighs the bias, and [Cannon2016Compression] prove that for $\\lambda < 2.17$ the perimeter stays at least a constant fraction of its maximum. This is the same balance of energy against counting that physicists use for phase transitions.",
        "why_it_matters": "It explains why 'prefer neighbours' is not enough on its own and how strong the preference must be."
      }
    ]
  },
  "history": "Compression introduced the **stochastic** approach to programmable matter, where the question is which bias produces which emergent behaviour [Cannon2016Compression].",
